  "answer_text": "Treating arbitrary _meta as authoritative can cause collisions with protocol-level metadata and lead implementations to make incorrect assumptions about reserved keys, producing unexpected or unsafe behavior. Implementations MUST NOT make assumptions about values at reserved _meta keys, should validate and only honor well-defined metadata, and must respect MCP’s reserved-prefix rules (any prefix containing 'modelcontextprotocol' or 'mcp').",
  "source_quote": "The `_meta` property/parameter is reserved by MCP to allow clients and servers to attach additional metadata to their interactions. Certain key names are reserved by MCP for protocol-level metadata, as specified below; implementations MUST NOT make assumptions about values at these keys. ... Any prefix beginning with zero or more valid labels, followed by `modelcontextprotocol` or `mcp`, followed by any valid label, is **reserved** for MCP use."
}

{
  "answer_text": "An MCP client must treat any server discovered from a public or custom registry as untrusted until it is vetted, and enforce endpoint and identity validation, strong authentication/authorization (least-privilege tokens or mTLS), runtime sandboxing and limits for third‑party tools, allowlist/approval flows for new entries, explicit consent and data‑flow restrictions for sending PII or sensitive content, and logging/audit and revocation/quarantine capabilities. These safeguards prevent misconfiguration, impersonation, unexpected capabilities, data exfiltration, and operational surprises that can arise because registry discovery provides availability but not inherent trust. Enforcing these controls at vetting time and at runtime ensures least privilege, limits blast radius, and preserves auditability and operator control.",
  "source_quote": "Central catalog of publicly available MCP servers for discovery and implementation"
}

{
  "answer_text": "If a client or registry UI assumes the registry always returns the latest-compatible versions or relies on cached server metadata, it can encounter runtime incompatibilities (broken schemas, changed method signatures, auth/contract changes), silent capability mismatches, and loss of reproducibility when pinned workloads suddenly break. To remain robust, clients should request and validate explicit version identifiers or ranges (don’t rely on an implicit “latest”), expose and allow pinning of all available versions in UIs, perform schema/feature validation and capability negotiation at runtime, use cache TTLs and revalidation tied to server-provided version IDs, and treat custom endpoints as independent authorities that require full version and capability checks. These practices prevent unexpected breakages from silent upgrades or stale metadata and give operators control over when to opt into newer versions.",
  "source_quote": "Show only latest versions"
}

{
  "answer_text": "An MCP client should anticipate that the Official MCP Registry or its UI can be slow or unavailable — for example the UI may show “Loading servers...”. Resilient behavior therefore includes surfacing a loading state to users and offering a “Retry” action so operators can re-attempt discovery when the registry becomes responsive.",
  "source_quote": "Loading servers...\n\nRetry"
