  "answer_text": "Realistic failures happen when SDKs or spec versions diverge in supported functionality or protocol features, causing connection failures, missing capabilities, or protocol errors when clients and servers interact. To prevent and recover from these interoperability failures, ensure all SDKs provide the same core functionality and full protocol support and build MCP clients that can connect to any MCP server (i.e., design for cross-SDK compatibility and full protocol coverage).",
  "source_quote": "All SDKs provide the same core functionality and full protocol support. Building MCP clients that can connect to any MCP server"
}

{
  "answer_text": "MCP should keep server and client roles distinct to enforce clear trust, operational, and deployment boundaries: servers mediate access control, auditing, resource normalization, and backend integration while clients focus on orchestration, UI, and agent logic, enabling reuse and least-privilege security. Collapsing those roles into a monolith increases coupling and blast radius, complicates deployment and upgrades, makes auditing, tenant isolation and access control harder, and forces mixed responsibilities for protocol/version compatibility. Operational consequences include higher latency and larger fault domains, harder horizontal scaling, and more complex CI/CD.",
  "source_quote": "Create MCP servers to expose your data and tools"
}
